8. JAPANESE CALLIGRAPHY — POETIC TITLE & INVOCATION

At the top‑centre of the map (at 960px, 20px), floating within the silver mist above Moonlit Blade Peak, is a bold, vertical column of Japanese calligraphy in gyōsho (semi‑cursive) script, using an ink of crimson‑flecked obsidian that shimmers with the dark light of the Higanbana.

Column 1 (Main Title, 16 characters) :

text
四つの里 — 影と紅と呪と鬼の隠れ谷
[Four Villages — The Hidden Valley of Shadow, Scarlet, Onmyōdō, and Demon]

Column 2 (Poetic Invocation, right side, 14 characters) :

text
影は高みに座し、花は深淵に咲き、巫女は門を守り、鬼は闇に吼ゆ
[The shadow sits on high, the flower blooms in the abyss, the maiden guards the gate, the demon roars in darkness.]

Column 3 (Poetic Invocation, left side, 12 characters) :

text
四つの道は交わらず、されど一つに連なる
[The four paths do not cross, yet they are connected as one.]

Column 4 (Secondary warning, smaller, 10 characters) :

text
地図に非ず — 見た者は黙せよ
[Not a map — those who see it shall remain silent.]

A tiny vermilion woodblock‑stamp English translation appears in delicate brackets directly below each column. The vermilion stamps are matte and slightly chalky, each stamp a perfect 4mm square with a stylised character for “seal” (印). There are four stamps – one for each sect – placed at the four corners of the calligraphy block.

Calligraphic texture details :

Subtle embossment of each character (visible as a faint raised edge)

Visible ink‑squeeze at stroke terminations (the “whisker” effect of sumi‑e)

Visible, mist‑stained, warm paper fibre beneath (very fine pale lines)

The vermilion stamps are matte and slightly chalky, the red a stark, beautiful contrast against the dark palette

