8. JAPANESE CALLIGRAPHY — POETIC TITLE & INVOCATION

At the top‑centre of the map (at 960px, 15px), floating within the silver mist, is a bold, vertical column of Japanese calligraphy in gyōsho (semi‑cursive) script, using an ink of silver‑flecked obsidian that shimmers like moonlight on black steel.

Column 1 (Main Title, 12 characters) :

text
影の里 — 夜の高き砦
[Shadow Village — The High Citadel of Night]

Column 2 (Poetic Invocation, right side, 14 characters) :

text
鷹は舞い降り、刃は闇に閃き、五つの影は語らう
[The falcon descends, the blade flashes in the darkness, the five shadows confer.]

Column 3 (Poetic Invocation, left side, 12 characters) :

text
忍びの道は静寂なり — されど、その一撃は雷
[The ninja’s path is silence — yet, that single strike is thunder.]

Column 4 (Secondary warning, smaller, 10 characters) :

text
見る者は黙せよ — 語る者は既に影の中
[Those who see, remain silent — those who speak are already in the shadows.]

A tiny vermilion woodblock‑stamp English translation appears in delicate brackets directly below each column. There are three stamps – one for the Shadow Sect (shuriken symbol), one for Hayabusa (falcon), and one for the Elders (crescent moon).

Calligraphic texture details :

Subtle embossment of each character

Visible ink‑squeeze at stroke terminations (the “whisker” effect)

Visible, mist‑stained, warm paper fibre beneath

The vermilion stamps are matte and slightly chalky, the red a stark contrast against the dark palette

